2. Installation and troubleshooting

Step 1 — Update the Linux system

The installation began by refreshing the package index and upgrading installed packages. The terminal requested the student account password for elevated privileges. Command: sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

Step 2 — Run the Hermes installer

The initial installation command was run as the regular user: curl -fsSL https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/install.sh | bash. The installation did not complete successfully.

Step 3 — Retry with a specified commit

Following the installation error, the team attempted a specific commit described in the source as v0.22.2. The commands were: curl -fsSL https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/install.sh -o /tmp/hermes-install.sh; bash /tmp/hermes-install.sh --commit d923a5e. The report then records SETUPTOOLS_USE_DISTUTILS=local followed by bash /tmp/hermes-install.sh as a setuptools troubleshooting attempt. These are documented as separate shell commands; the source does not independently prove that the environment variable remained set for the subsequent command.

3. Initial setup selections

Step 4 — Begin interactive setup

After the installer proceeded, the interactive prompts were completed. The team selected Yes to review import options and then Quick Setup.

Step 5 — Configure runtime and optional services

The local terminal backend was retained. Messaging-platform setup was skipped, gateway installation was declined, and the optional usage-sharing prompt was answered No thanks.

4. Language model provider configuration

Step 6 — Select the provider

When Hermes reported that no inference provider was configured, the team chose to set one up and selected Custom (Direct API) for the Ollama-compatible service.

Step 7 — Enter the API endpoint

The source report records the API base URL as http://136.160.215.236:11434/v1. The setup next prompted for an API key. The key itself is not included in this report.

Figure 15. Custom OpenAI-compatible API base URL prompt.

Step 8 — Select compatibility and model.

The API compatibility mode was set to 1 (Auto-detect). The available-model list included qwen3.8:27b, which the team identified as its selected model.

Step 9 — Complete model settings

The context-length field was left blank to use auto-detection. The display name was set to Ollama, and the reasoning effort was set to Medium.

5. Functional verification

Step 10 — Launch and test Hermes

Hermes displayed its welcome screen. The team tested the installation by asking for the present working directory with pwd. The first test returned an error stating that provider auto had no endpoint configured, so the request was not sent.

Step 11 — Restart and retest

The session was closed and Hermes was launched again. The subsequent screen showed Hermes Agent v0.23.1, model qwen3.8:27b, and provider CUSTOM. The pwd test then returned /home/student, demonstrating that the agent could execute a basic terminal command in the recorded session.

6. Results and limitations

The documented workflow shows an initial installation failure, a commit-specific retry and setuptools troubleshooting, interactive configuration of a custom Ollama-compatible model endpoint, and an unsuccessful first functional test. After restarting Hermes, the working-directory command succeeded and returned /home/student.

Version reporting requires care: the source associates commit d923a5e with v0.22.2, while the later Hermes welcome screen reports v0.23.1. This report preserves both observations rather than assuming they refer to the same installed build. The screenshots demonstrate basic command execution, but do not independently establish network isolation, the complete permissions inventory, or a comprehensive audit of file changes and tool calls.

7. Evidence handling and reproducibility

For the final project repository, retain the installation commands, sanitized configuration information, version output, and screenshots in environment/. Do not publish API keys, access tokens, or other credentials. The screenshots in this report document the actions visible during setup; additional command logs and configuration records would be needed to establish a complete, reproducible audit trail.

